class:: SpecHFC
summary:: Frequency weighted spectral content
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIRa, Classes/SpecCentroid2, Classes/SpecFlatness2

description::
Returns sum(f * w) for linear weighting, or sum(f.squared * w) for quadratic weighting. Frequencies are in Hz, and no level normalization is applied. With power weights the units are raw FFT power times Hz or Hz squared.

For these formulas w is the raw FFT magnitude or its square, and f is the bin-center frequency in Hz. Silence returns 0. The reader preserves the FFT chain. High-frequency-content descriptor family introduced in onset work by Masri and Bateman (1996); MIRa makes Hz weighting and magnitude/power conventions explicit.

CPU cost is linear in the number of FFT bins; no memory is allocated while processing. It adds no lookahead beyond the upstream FFT. At a 1024-sample window and 48 kHz the initial analysis window is about 21.3 ms. Changing FFT size or window affects unnormalized values.

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecCentroid::, this reads FFT frames and produces control-rate timbre information; this class measures frequency weighted spectral content instead of the average frequency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: chain
FFT chain from an FFT with the same literal fftSize. New-frame values update the output; between frames the previous output is retained.

argument:: weighting
Literal \linear weights by Hz; \quadratic weights by Hz squared.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included bin center in Hz; default 0 includes DC.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included bin center in Hz; 0 selects Nyquist. Empty ranges return 0.

argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; literal 1 uses squared magnitude (power).

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. Must match the FFT buffer.

returns::
One control-rate descriptor value.

examples::
SpecHFC computes high-frequency-content energy by weighting spectral bins by their frequency. In percussion processing, HFC rises sharply on cymbal and hi-hat transients. In this example, HFC controls an automatic high-frequency transient gate, routing cymbal shimmer to an airy stereo chorus while preserving clean dry bass and snare. An interactive code::MIRaGUI.scope:: displays real-time high-frequency content.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIRaGUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~miraBuffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~miraBus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~miraExample = {
        var source = if(~miraBuffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~miraBuffer, BufRateScale.kr(~miraBuffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var hfc = SpecHFC.kr(chain, weighting: \linear, minFreq: 1000, maxFreq: 18000, spectrumMode: 0, fftSize: 1024);
        var gate = (hfc > 300).lag(0.005, 0.08);
        var cymbalWash = HPF.ar(source, 4000) * gate;
        var chorus = DelayC.ar(cymbalWash, 0.02, SinOsc.kr([0.5, 0.7]).range(0.002, 0.008)) * 0.3;
        var sound = (source * 0.25 ! 2) + chorus;

        if(~miraBus.notNil) { Out.kr(~miraBus, (hfc / 10).clip(0, 1000)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~miraGUI = MIRaGUI.scope("High Frequency Content", ~miraBus, 0.0, 500.0, "HFC");
});
)
::
code::
(
~miraExample.free;
if(~miraGUI.notNil) { ~miraGUI.close };
if(~miraBus.notNil) { ~miraBus.free };
if(~miraBuffer.notNil) { ~miraBuffer.free };
)
::
